    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A mental model for visualizing this layering is to think of it like a meticulously constructed, multi-tiered **Lego tower**, where the root is the foundational baseplate. Each morpheme—the case marker, the possessive, the particle—is a specific, interlocking brick that must snap onto the previous one in the correct order.\n\nYou can teach students to picture the process as a sequence of \"assembly steps,\" rather than a single monolithic word. Start with the base (the root), and then sequentially add the bricks (the suffixes), checking at each step if the new brick fits the color and shape of the layer beneath it (the morphophonemic constraints like vowel harmony).\n\nThis tower model emphasizes **linearity** and **dependence**: the top brick can't be placed until the one below it is stable, just as a suffix cannot attach correctly until the preceding morpheme has been fully realized phonologically.\n\nThis visual metaphor helps move students away from seeing the word as a jumble of letters and toward seeing it as a structured, algorithmic construction. It makes the abstract concept of agglutination tangible.",
